Assume you have an interface called Payable as follows:
public interface Payable {
  double getPaymentAmount();
}
If you have another class Invoice that implements this interface and getQuantity() and getPricePerItem() methods that return quantity and price per item, which options correctly implement the Payable contract? Treat each option as an independent class skeleton with accessible getQuantity() and getPricePerItem() methods supplied and compatible with multiplication returning double. An abstract class may supply a concrete implementation. Select all that apply.

options.text:
public abstract class Invoice implements Payable {
  public double getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}

public class Invoice implements Payable {
  public double getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}

public class Invoice implements Payable {
  public getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}

public class Invoice extends Payable {
  public double getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}

answer.text:
public abstract class Invoice implements Payable {
  public double getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}; public class Invoice implements Payable {
  public double getPaymentAmount() {
    return getQuantity() * getPricePerItem();
  }
}

